README.OSK -- utree 3.03b-um on OS-9/68000ORIG/ is utree as comp.sources.unix posted it, volume 26 issues 64 to 71(September 1992), flattened into one directory: Peter Klingebiel'sscreen-oriented file manager, "a portable Unix version of the DOSXTREE", with Paul Vixie's moderator note calling it "dired-likefunctionality without the overhead of GNU Emacs".The distribution already ports itself eighteen ways -- ORIG/conf.h.BSD,conf.h.V.2, conf.h.SCO, conf.h.AIX, conf.h.SUN, conf.h.MIPS and the rest,each with a Makefile beside it -- so OS-9 joins that seam rather thancutting a new one.  Every change below is inside an `#ifdef OSK'.THE CONFIGURATIONconf.h       UTLIB is /dd/SYS/UTREE, which is where the startup file, the             key bindings and the help pages live.  NOWINCHG, because             OS-9 has no SIGWINCH and no window-size call: the screen is             whatever the termcap says.  UTCLOCK off, because the clock             in the corner is redrawn from SIGALRM and OS-9 has no alarm             signal either.             NODIRENT IS NOT DEFINED, AND THAT ONE COST AN EVENING.  It             looks like the right switch for a system without <dirent.h>,             and it is a trap: it makes `opendir(n)' into             `fopen(n, "r")' and reads directory entries as a struct             with a d_ino in it.  RBF will not open a directory as a             plain file -- E$FNA -- so every directory came back as             "Cannot read directory" and utree drew an empty tree.  OS-9             HAS the real opendir/readdir/closedir in unix.l, and its own             <dir.h> says in its first line that "the method and names             work like the BSD4.2 directory calls", so this is the BSD             path: defs.h declares them and util.c takes the BSD arm.cmds.h       The default editor, pager, hex dumper and shell, named for             what this collection carries: vi, less, dump and ksh, where             the System V arm says ed, pg, od and sh.  `rmdir' is not             here, so Rmdir runs `rm -d' -- which removes what is under             the directory too, but utree refuses a directory holding             subdirectories and empties the files itself first, so by the             time it runs there is nothing left for the difference to             reach.  `du' here spells "just the summary" -a, not -s.THE TERMINALterm.c       OS-9 has no termio and no sgtty: the terminal's settings are             a `struct _sgs' read with _gs_opt() and written with             _ss_opt() (<sgstat.h>).  Raw mode clears echo, auto line             feed, the page pause, the four line-editing characters, the             end-of-record and end-of-file characters, the pause             character and the two keyboard signal characters.  ESC is             OS-9's end-of-file character on a terminal and utree uses             ESC, which is why that one matters.  enablesignals() puts             the interrupt and abort characters back and nothing else;             terminalreset() puts the whole record back, so nothing             outlives the program.             THE KEYBOARD READS ONE BYTE AT A TIME, with read(), not             getc(stdin).  getc() is a buffered read, and with the             end-of-record character cleared the driver has nothing to             end a record on -- so it waits for a full buffer and the             program looks dead from the first keystroke.             keypressed() is _gs_rdy(), OS-9's SS_Ready: how many             characters are waiting.  That is what FIONREAD was for, and             ORIG/fionread.c is the distribution's own test for it.             PC, UP and BC: termlib defines UP and BC and expects the             program to define PC.  Declaring all three, as the Unix arms             do, is a double definition here -- l68 has no common             symbols.defs.h       The OSK include arm: <sys/sysmacros.h>, <sgstat.h>, <dir.h>.             NOT <time.h> -- <sgstat.h> defines `struct sgtbuf' and the             SDK's <time.h> defines it again with no guard, and ctime()             there is a MACRO, so it cannot be declared either.FILE STATUS             OS-9 packs file type and permissions into ONE BYTE, so the             Unix reading of st_mode is wrong in every part:               S_IFMT is 0xff and S_IFDIR is 0x80 -- a FLAG, not a value               a type field can equal.  STFMT() masks with S_IFDIR here,               and S_IFREG is 0, since a regular file is simply one that               is not a directory.  With the Unix reading, `st_mode &               S_IFMT' returns the permission bits as well and no file               matches any type: utree started and stopped at once.               There are two permission sets, owner and public, not               three, and they are numbered the other way up: 1 read, 2               write, 4 execute.  The Stat panel shows the two rows, and               the short form is OS-9's own attribute string, `d-ewrewr',               in the order `attr' prints it.  Set-user-ID, set-group-ID               and the sticky bit do not exist; defined as 0 they made               `(mode & S_ISUID) == S_ISUID' true for every file, so the               panel announced "Set user ID on execution" on all of them.               OS-9's seventh bit is `single user' instead.               A file is owned by a NUMBER PAIR, group.user, and there is               no group database and no name: chown() takes the pair as               one long.  The panel shows `0.0 (group.user)' and takes a               new owner the same way; the Group command is gone, because               there is nothing for it to look up.               st_dev is a `char *' -- the SDK's <UNIX/stat.h> says "a               pointer to the device name" -- so the major/minor split is               meaningless, and there is no /dev to search for a match.               The device is read off the front of the pathname instead.osk.c (new)  getgrgid() and getgrnam() answer "no such group", which is             the truth; getpwnam() is spelled getpwname() in blarslib.             And the two that matter:             system() IS NOT THE WAY TO RUN A COMMAND FROM A PORT             HERE.  This C library's system() forks the bare module name             `shell' and never reads $SHELL.  On your own OS-9 system             that is exactly right: `shell' is yours, it is always             there, and SYS/login loads it off /h1 so the programs that             shell out work.  But utree should not have to depend on             that -- with the collection standing by itself there is no             `shell' module, and system() then returns a nonsense value             having printed not one character (measured 2026-09-19:             677130, no output, with blarslib and without).             DOC/README-SHELLS has the whole measurement.             There is no fork()/exec() pair to fall back on either:             F$Fork starts a NEW process from a module name, with             nothing for an exec to overwrite.  So the shell is forked             here BY THE NAME UTREE'S OWN `SHELL' VARIABLE HOLDS, with             os9exec(os9fork, ...), and waited for -- the way perl's             SRC/perl4/osk.c does it and Bob Larson's blarslib popen             before that.  That variable starts at $SHELL, and the             startup file and the `=' screen can both change it.             THE `-c' IS LOAD-BEARING.  ksh here does accept a whole             command line as its single argument, and for a MODULE that             works -- but a shell PROCEDURE FILE invoked that way runs             with no arguments at all: `ksh "argprobe a b"' left $# at 0             and put the whole string in $0, where `ksh -c "argprobe a b"'             passed both.  utree's Backup command runs a procedure file.             oskpopen()/oskpclose() are the same fork with /pipe put             where the child's standard output goes -- the Info panel             wants one, for the kilobytes a subtree occupies.hist.c       callshell() and callsystem() call oskspawn() where the Unix             arms fork and execlp.  Interrupt and quit are still trapped;             OS-9 has no SIGTERM, so main.c does not arm one.tree.c       The Info panel reads du's answer through oskpopen().  This             du opens with a BLANK LINE and then says "Total of 25             kBytes (100 sectors) in 1 directories and 3 files.", so the             number wanted is the first run of digits anywhere in the             output rather than at the front of the first line, and the             panel is labelled kBytes.getopt.c     Carried from the distribution's sup/ and NOT in the recipe:             blarslib has a getopt and it parses utree's options             correctly, so the spare is here for reference rather than             compiled in.  Build it in if a library of yours has none.WHAT IS INSTALLED BESIDE THE PROGRAMSYS/UTREE/utree             The startup file.  utree reads $HOME/.utree first and this             only if there is none, so a copy in your own home directory             overrides it.  Every command it names is on this disk; the             example file in ORIG names a dozen that are not (ps, rwho,             ruptime, df, pg, f77, pc, pcat, zcat).SYS/UTREE/utree-xterm-256color             Key bindings, named for $TERM -- utree looks for             `utree-$TERM', and SYS/login sets TERM to xterm-256color.             Arrows, TAB and the letter commands need nothing here; these             are the keypad and function keys, which no termcap             describes.  A terminal of your own wants a copy under its             own name.SYS/UTREE/utree.help             The help pages, as posted.  `H' reads them.SYS/UTREE/utree.backup  (new, ours)             The Backup command runs the program BACKUP names, and the             distribution ships one that writes to a tape with cpio             (ORIG/utree.backup): /dev/rst0, /dev/ris0, /dev/rmt/ctape0.             None of that is here.  This one archives the list utree             wrote with `tar', which is the nearest thing this disk has             to the same job.  Neither tar here reads a list of names             from a file -- the small tar takes `[ctx][mfv] tarfile             file...' and gtar answers "can't add file -T" -- so the list             is expanded onto the command line.             It is named in full in the startup file because SYS/UTREE is             not on PATH, and it ships with the execute bit set, which             took a line in tools/mktar.py: that tool marks a file             executable by its DIRECTORY, and nothing outside CMDS had             ever needed to be.MEASURED 2026-09-19 under os9exec, with the disk as /dd and /h0:  utree /dd/DOC reads 303 directories and 1553 files and draws the tree;  `>' and `<' cross between the tree pane and the file pane;  `H' pages the help, `=' the variables -- which show the startup file's     SHELL=ksh, EDITOR=vi, PAGER=less, XDUMPER=dump;  `i' answers `Access:d-ewrewr kBytes:6617 Files:42 Dirs:8' for /dd/SYS;  `s' shows the device as /dd, the owner as 0.0, the two permission rows     and `d-ewrewr (octal: 0277)';  `v' pages a file through less;  `l' lists by pattern;  `t' and `T' tag, and the tags show as `+';  `!' escapes to a command line, runs it, keeps it in the history and     recalls it with `!' again;  `b' writes the list and calls utree.backup, which archived four files;  `Q' then `y' leaves, and the terminal is as it was.